<!--
    decbench cases-missing pool (coreutils timeout/split main, bash 7 functions,
    tar 3, grep, openssh scp/sftp/sftp-server, coreutils -O2 ls): kuna emitted
    NO OUTPUT AT ALL for these functions - the whole decompilation died and the
    user got an error record instead of C.

    Root cause: P3 carried two hand-maintained copies of the OpCode -> TypeOp
    mapping (`glb->inst[opc]`), one per rule-batch file, each a partial subset
    with a `panic!` default arm.  When a simplification rule retyped an op to an
    op-code its file's list did not name, the panic unwound the whole function.

    The shape below is the minimized form of the most common failure (24
    functions / 27 panic events in a 20,246-function sweep):

        int f(int a, int b) { return (a >> 3) & (b >> 3); }

    RuleBitUndistribute rewrites `(V s>> c) & (W s>> c)` to `(V & W) s>> c`,
    which re-sets an op's code to CPUI_INT_SRIGHT - an op-code missing from
    `ruleaction_2`'s whitelist, so `op_typeop` panicked
    (`ruleaction_2.rs:96`).  `ruleaction_3`'s separate whitelist was missing
    CPUI_FLOAT_INT2FLOAT / CPUI_FLOAT_LESS / CPUI_FLOAT_ADD and killed further
    functions (coreutils -O2 ls, betaflight ARM) the same way.

    Stage model: P3 dataflow, simplification-quiescence sub-stage.  Both seams
    now resolve through the canonical `inst[]` table
    (`p5_types/typeop.rs::seam_type_op_for`), which is total over every op-code
    (its `match` has no wildcard arm, so the compiler enforces completeness) and
    degrades instead of panicking on the CPUI_MAX sentinel.

    No option: this is a strict correctness fix (a function that produced
    nothing now produces C).  Measured on 12 desktop/embedded ELF binaries /
    15,596 functions: error records 21 -> 2 (the 2 left are a jump-table panic
    and a loader read, both owned elsewhere) and ZERO functions that already
    decompiled changed their emitted text.

    Single pass asserts the fixed pipeline: the function has a real body, and
    the shifts are undistributed exactly as upstream Ghidra does it.
-->
